rive to right the blue car which  is merging in front of the yellow it  speeds up so the yellow car doesn't have  to slow down so that's kind of the  desired polite maneuver  um but if you look at kind of the you  know state of the art in game theory  with state and input constraints that's  this open loop nash equilibrium and that  we've shown on the right basically the  car still merges but the blue doesn't  react so the yellow has to slow down and  that's because the  the open loop game doesn't capture this  key point that comes back comes through  feedback that what you assume the other  agent will do in reaction to what you're  doing that back and forth  okay so that's captured here and it's  what we do in real life in many  scenarios but it's not captured here the  yellow car like kind of the ego car here  we're thinking about has to slow down  this is um you know it's like that  hesitant behavior that you used to see  in autonomous vehicles when you know  there would be a car like stop it would  sort of stop even you know if a car was  slowing down in front of it so that that  you don't have a realistic assumption  about the interaction of the two  vehicles there's not that feedback  okay so  how did we start to think about this and  and getting towards like how you would  compute a feedback solution here so what  is uh is a little bit of an overview  here maybe captured stylistically so  let's suppose now okay so ego is the  blue vehicle and this red vehicle um it  may keep going straight or it may  change lanes so let's assume it's maybe  it's going to go straight with 80  percent prob or 0.8 probability and  change lanes with 0.2 probability so  what we do is um is we make two copies  of that vehicle one to represent one  hypothesis and one to represent the  other hypothesis so we kind of think  about this um  uh representation in terms of  multi like creating these kind of ghosts  vehicles that are going to represent  these different hypotheses we have ab